Does a status-4 "partial" encode's reported `length` match what is actually playable?

Measured on the truncated-but-structurally-valid mp4 ({GUID_PARTIAL_2}) — a 30 s
1280x720 h264 clip written with a faststart moov, then cut off at 56% of the file,
so the container header advertises 30 s of media that is not all present.

API video object said:

    length                30
    width x height        1280 x 720
    framerate             30
    availableResolutions  480p,720p,360p
    storageSize           16940354
    encodeProgress        100
    status                4

Master playlist (https://{PULL_ZONE_HOST}/{GUID_PARTIAL_2}/playlist.m3u8) — a full,
normal-looking ladder, no indication anything is wrong:

    #EXTM3U
    #EXT-X-VERSION:3

    #EXT-X-STREAM-INF:BANDWIDTH=590857,AVERAGE-BANDWIDTH=574582,CODECS="avc1.64001e,mp4a.40.2",RESOLUTION=640x360,CLOSED-CAPTIONS=NONE
    360p/video.m3u8
    #EXT-X-STREAM-INF:BANDWIDTH=1003168,AVERAGE-BANDWIDTH=973097,CODECS="avc1.64001f,mp4a.40.2",RESOLUTION=854x480,CLOSED-CAPTIONS=NONE
    480p/video.m3u8
    #EXT-X-STREAM-INF:BANDWIDTH=2986192,AVERAGE-BANDWIDTH=2960268,CODECS="avc1.64001f,mp4a.40.2",RESOLUTION=1280x720,CLOSED-CAPTIONS=NONE
    720p/video.m3u8

Summing EXTINF over the 360p media playlist:

    sum(EXTINF) = 16.70 s

So `length` is 30 and the video is 16.70 s. `length` is read from the source
container's declared duration, not from what the encoder produced, and on a damaged
source the two disagree by the whole missing tail (here 44%).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
`availableResolutions` ordering is not stable

Two encodes of the same truncated file, same library, minutes apart:

    run 1  ->  360p,480p,720p
    run 2  ->  480p,720p,360p

Same three renditions, different order. The success-path capture
(encode-status-progression.log) happened to fill in low-to-high, which reads like a
guarantee; it is not one. Parse this field as a set.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
`transcodingMessages[].value` is truncated by Bunny

The level-2 diagnostic's `value` carries raw encoder stderr and is cut mid-token at
255 characters ("...[h264 @ 0x...] Invalid N"). Do not expect to parse a complete
error out of it, and do not assume the last line is the significant one.
